course: dictionary
lesson: 4932
title: Impertinence and rude cheek
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Impertinence is a noun. It means rude behaviour or words that show no respect, especially to an older person or someone in charge. People often call it cheek. When someone says «What impertinence!», they mean the other person has been rude and disrespectful.
explain: Read the words around it: «first, of stealing a valuable motor-car; secondly, of driving to the public danger; and, thirdly, of gross impertinence to the rural police». The sentence lists three wrongs, and the rural police are people in charge. So impertinence is rude disrespect towards them. Try swapping in cheek: «gross cheek to the rural police» still makes sense.
explain: Impertinence can also mean one rude remark or act, so you can say «an impertinence». In Alice's Adventures in Wonderland, the creatures and royalty are offended by Alice's impertinence, but she is usually just being honest. The clues show who is offended, and that helps you see what the word means.
fact: Impertinence means rude behaviour or words that show no respect, often to someone older or in charge.
fact: Another everyday word for impertinence is cheek.
fact: Impertinence is mostly uncountable, but an impertinence is a single rude remark or act.
fact: Phrases such as «the impertinence of it!» and «What impertinence!» show that someone feels offended.
fact: Good check: swap in «rudeness» or «cheek» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «...gross impertinence to the rural police» What does impertinence mean in this sentence? | A long walk in the countryside | Helpful advice for the police | A kind present for the police | *Rude cheek towards the police
exercise: Someone says, «What impertinence!» What has happened? | Someone has lost their way | Someone has given a lovely gift | Someone has told a very long story | *Someone has been rude and shown no respect
exercise: Which word means about the same as impertinence in everyday talk? | kindness | silence | *cheek | patience
test: In Alice's Adventures in Wonderland, the creatures call Alice impertinent, though she is usually just honest. What does this suggest? | Honest people are never rude | *Being called impertinent is a judgement by others, and it is not always fair | Impertinence means being very quiet | Impertinence is always kind and helpful
test: Which sentence uses impertinence to mean one rude remark? | The impertinence of the sunny morning made everyone smile. | Impertinence is a word that has four letters. | *She was annoyed by his impertinence, a rude comment about her hair. | He went to the shop for impertinence and milk.
exam: What does impertinence mean in the phrase «What impertinence!»? | A very long journey | *Rude behaviour or words that show no respect | A type of hat worn by police officers | Kind and generous behaviour
